#!/bin/sh
# CAPABILITY-LAYER SHIM — process termination
#
# See .claude/shims/README.md. In short: the text guard inspects the command
# string an agent asks to run, and ten adversarial rounds proved that boundary is
# structurally bypassable, because the shell rewrites the text before it resolves
# the program. This shim sits after that rewrite — every quote, caret, variable
# and redirect is already gone by the time PATH is searched, so all those
# spellings arrive here identically.
#
# The 2026-08-23 incident command was `taskkill /PID 39564 /T /F`, which never
# mentions codex. It terminated the reviewer's own process tree while the
# pipeline still exited 0, recording a clean security gate that never ran. So
# termination is refused by CAPABILITY, not by target.

NAME="$(basename "$0")"
cat >&2 <<REFUSED
CAPABILITY SHIM: refusing process termination via \`$NAME\`.

An agent force-terminating processes is how the 2026-08-23 Codex reviewer
destroyed its own review: \`taskkill /PID 39564 /T /F\` — a command that never
mentions codex — while the pipeline still exited 0 with no verdict.

To stop something you started, use the harness (TaskStop). If a stray process
genuinely must be terminated, tell Mason the PID and why and let him do it: a
wrong PID here ends the thing that was proving the work.
REFUSED
exit 9
